{
  "id": "ticket_01KYAW7DSFYVNM7NP12J9CYJDZ",
  "name": "start/takeover: eliminate the residual new-session orphan window with a write-ahead journal (currently detect+repair only)",
  "slug": "start-takeover-eliminate-the-residual",
  "spec": {
    "summary": "start/takeover: eliminate the residual new-session orphan window with a write-ahead journal (currently detect+repair only)",
    "details_md": "ticket_01KYAPKRJ9RJRJRAV42WCTJET4 fixed the takeover write order (the OLD/previous session is now closed BEFORE the ticket write, the point of no return) and added orphan detection+repair (slop reindex --heal). One window remains, structurally: start always creates the BRAND NEW session file before the ticket write that references it (the ticket needs the session's freshly-minted id), so a crash between createSession and updateTicket still leaves that new session unreferenced (ended_at: null, detectable/repairable via reindex --heal, but not prevented). Eliminating this outright would need a write-ahead journal or two-phase commit around the whole start transaction -- explicitly out of scope for the crash-window ticket per the write-order-fix-plus-repair-path scope the human coordinator set.",
    "acceptance": [
      "design a journal/two-phase-commit approach for start's multi-write transaction (or an equivalent mechanism) that closes the window entirely, not just detects it after the fact",
      "either implement it, or record a considered decision not to (e.g. detect+repair is judged sufficient long-term) with rationale"
    ],
